  pages={1-5},
  abstract={Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is a growing global health concern, requiring early detection and precise prediction for effective intervention. This study proposes a novel hybrid framework leveraging Federated Learning (FL), Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), and Multi-modal Data Fusion to predict CKD progression while addressing challenges such as privacy, data imbalance, and multi-modal integration. The suggested techniques use cutting-edge deep learning models, such as Tversky loss to enhance prediction performance on unbalanced datasets and a CNN-RBM hybrid architecture for imaging data. The KiTS19 imaging dataset, the UCI CKD dataset, and a synthetic multi-modal dataset were used in the experiments. With an impressive 94.1% accuracy, 93.6% precision, 94.8% recall, and an AUC of 0.96 on the UCI CKD dataset, the CNN-RBM hybrid model outperformed more conventional models like logistic regression (85.5%) and random forests (89.4%). With a Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC) of 93.7% and a Tversky Index of 0.96, the suggested CNNRBM model surpassed cutting-edge U-Net models for imaging data. Federated Learning, which was used to protect data privacy, demonstrated its feasibility for decentralized training with a comparable accuracy of 92.7%. Serum creatinine, blood pressure, and albumin levels were found to be the most significant characteristics using explainable AI methods like SHAP, guaranteeing clinical interpretability and reliability. The drawbacks of single-modality techniques were addressed by multi-modal integration, which further improved model accuracy and resilience by merging structured data, imagery, and wearable device data. These findings show that the suggested approach is extremely suitable to real-world CKD prediction scenarios since it not only provides state-of-the-art accuracy but also guarantees data privacy and interpretability. By offering practical insights into CKD progression and risk factors, this work expands the boundaries of AI-driven healthcare.},
